1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:04,360
The first of the anemias that we're going to discuss are microcytic anemias.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:07,840
In a microcytic anemia, the red blood cells are small.

3
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:14,400
And this size is actually measured by the MCV, or the mean corpuscular volume.

4
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:18,360
And so the MCV in a microcytic anemia will be less than 80.

5
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,820
Recall that the normal MCV is anywhere between 80 to 100.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:26,160
And so in a small, when the cells are small, the MCV will be less than 80.

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:32,360
It is useful to have a little bit of an understanding of how the microcytosis occurs.

8
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:39,160
Because once you understand how the microcytosis occurs, you'll get a better understanding of the causes of microcytic anemia.

9
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:47,080
So let's go back and remind ourselves that the red blood cell is actually formed from a progenitor cell called the erythroblast.

10
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:51,120
The erythroblast is basically the cell that produces the red blood cells.

11
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:55,780
Now relatively speaking, the erythroblast is a large cell.

12
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:03,680
And it will divide, and then divide, and divide, and divide, and eventually it will produce small red blood cells.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,580
So for example, if this is the erythroblast, it will divide into two cells.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,060
And then it will divide, these two cells will each divide into cells, etc.

15
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:17,900
And so with each division, the cells become smaller.

16
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:25,500
And that's important when understanding a microcytic anemia, because it helps us to understand why the microcytosis is occurring.

17
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:36,420
So in particular, we should recognize that microcytic anemias occur because there is an extra division of the cell.

18
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:40,720
I.e., the erythroblast undergoes one extra division.

19
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:47,200
Or why don't we say that the cells, as they're generating from the erythroblast to become mature red blood cells,

20
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:49,440
they sort of undergo one extra division.

21
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:51,660
Now the next question we could ask is why.

22
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:54,080
Why would the red blood cells undergo an extra division?

23
00:01:54,080 --> 00:02:03,140
And the answer, at least the way I think about it, is that microcytic anemia is always a problem of decreased production of hemoglobin.

24
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:08,580
So in each of the microcytic anemias, the problem is the decreased production of hemoglobin.

25
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:12,980
Now as the red blood cells are developing, if there is not enough hemoglobin,

26
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:19,400
one way by which the red blood cells can try to maintain the concentration of hemoglobin within the red blood cell

27
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,280
would be to divide one extra time.

28
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:30,160
Another way to think about this is that it is as if the red blood cell is trying to produce a nice, pretty, red-pink cell.

29
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:35,220
And if there is not enough hemoglobin, there will not be enough pink in the cell.

30
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:40,440
And so the red blood cell will divide one extra time in order to try to maintain its color,

31
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,260
i.e., the concentration of hemoglobin.

32
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:47,420
Okay, now with that in mind, you then have to understand a little bit of biochemistry

33
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:49,560
to understand the microcytic anemias.

34
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:51,820
So let's remind ourselves, what is hemoglobin?

35
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:53,320
Well, the name gives it away.

36
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:55,860
It's heme plus globin.

37
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:58,340
All right, and what is heme?

38
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:03,540
Heme is basically iron and protoporphyrin.

39
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:09,260
And so if I were to create just a simple understanding in your mind by doing the following,

40
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:17,750
I've told you that hemoglobin, we know that it equals heme plus globin.

41
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:26,560
All right, and you know that heme is essentially composed of iron plus protoporphyrin.

42
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,100
I'm just going to say proto here, P-R-O-T-O.

43
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:36,080
Then, if you understand this basic biochemistry, it's not hard to recognize the causes of microcytic anemia.

44
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:41,540
For example, let's say that there is a lack of iron, which is called iron deficiency.

45
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:45,040
If there's low iron, then there would be low heme.

46
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,880
And if there's low heme, there'll be low hemoglobin.

47
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:51,060
And if there's low hemoglobin, there'll be a microcytic anemia.

48
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:54,580
So one cause of a microcytic anemia is iron deficiency anemia.

49
00:03:55,580 --> 00:04:00,400
Another way by which we could get microcytic anemia would be, let's say that there's iron,

50
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:07,300
but the iron cannot be used because the patient has a chronic inflammatory state,

51
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:10,180
and therefore the iron is being stored away in the macrophages.

52
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:16,000
So when iron is locked away in the macrophages, that is called anemia of chronic disease.

53
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:19,300
And the idea there is that iron is not available for use.

54
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:23,740
And so therefore, if there's low iron, then there's going to be low heme.

55
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:26,740
And if there's low heme, then there's going to be low hemoglobin.

56
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:30,180
And if there's low hemoglobin, there's going to be a microcytic anemia.

57
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:34,540
And so the second cause of microcytic anemia is actually anemia of chronic disease.

58
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:40,160
The third cause is any circumstance that would decrease the production of protoporphyrin.

59
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:46,560
And so the disease in which, or the anemia in which there is a decreased production of protoporphyrin,

60
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:48,460
is called sideroblastic anemia.

61
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:53,800
And so in sideroblastic anemia, there's low protoporphyrin, which results in low heme.

62
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:56,240
Low heme results in low hemoglobin.

63
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,640
And low hemoglobin then results in a microcytic anemia.

64
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:04,920
And then finally, the fourth cause of microcytic anemia is thalassemia.

65
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:06,060
And what's thalassemia?

66
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:10,460
Thalassemia is when there's decreased production of the globin chain.

67
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:14,880
Obviously, decreased production of the globin chain would result in decreased hemoglobin,

68
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:18,120
and decreased hemoglobin would result in a microcytic anemia.

69
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:22,560
So actually, this is not hard, if you understand a little bit of basic biochemistry.

70
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:26,200
And there really are four major causes of microcytic anemia.

71
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:32,680
Iron deficiency anemia, anemia of chronic disease, sideroblastic anemia, and thalassemia.

72
00:05:33,380 --> 00:05:37,060
Now that's the global picture that you have to have sort of in your mind.

73
00:05:37,060 --> 00:05:42,180
And if you keep this picture in your mind, you'll be very comfortable when dealing with microcytic anemias.

74
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:46,080
There are, of course, some details that we have to be familiar with.

75
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:55,860
And so we're going to spend the remainder of our time in this section now walking through each of these anemias individually and highlighting some important high yield principles.

76
00:05:55,860 --> 00:06:00,860
So the first of the anemias that we want to discuss is iron deficiency anemia.

77
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:07,600
Iron deficiency anemia is due to decreased levels of iron within the body.

78
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:10,440
This is the most common type of anemia.

79
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:14,800
Lack of iron is actually the most common nutritional deficiency in the world.

80
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:22,080
The WHO estimates that approximately one-third of the entire world's population is deficient in iron.

81
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:28,000
And so iron deficiency anemia is something that all of us are going to encounter in our patients.

82
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:30,620
Now a little bit about the biochemistry of iron.

83
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:35,120
You do need to understand it to understand some of the high yields for this disease.

84
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:42,080
Iron is consumed in both heme, which is meat-derived, and non-heme, which is vegetable-derived, forms.

85
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:46,440
And the heme form, i.e. the meat-derived form, is more readily absorbed.

86
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,540
Absorption, of course, occurs in the duodenum.

87
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:50,800
You do need to know that.

88
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:52,460
Absorption occurs in the duodenum.

89
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,580
And it's the enterocyte.

90
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:59,180
Recall that the enterocyte histologically is the cell that lines the villi of the duodenum.

91
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,760
The enterocyte regulates the absorption of iron.

92
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:06,620
Basically, the enterocyte takes up iron from the gut

93
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:14,380
and then has the ability to transport it into the blood via a transporter called ferroportin.

94
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:16,380
Now let me draw this for you.

95
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:18,920
Let's pretend that this is the enterocyte.

96
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:21,720
And this is the lumen of the gut.

97
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:23,820
And over here would be a blood vessel.

98
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:28,020
The enterocyte basically takes up iron from the gut.

99
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,200
And then it makes a decision.

100
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:35,220
It decides, should I send iron into the blood or not?

101
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:44,620
The transporter that it uses to move iron from the enterocyte into the blood is called ferroportin.

102
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:46,680
Ferroportin.

103
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:50,760
And this is a key regulatory step in iron biochemistry.

104
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:56,440
The idea here is that the body has the ability to take up iron

105
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:59,520
but does not have the ability to get rid of iron.

106
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:03,140
There really is no way for the body to get rid of iron.

107
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:07,600
Now it's true that a little bit of iron gets lost when the skin is turning over.

108
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,560
And so as skin sloughs off the human being, iron is lost.

109
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:12,920
A tiny bit of iron is lost.

110
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:16,940
And it's also true that a female loses iron through the menstrual cycle.

111
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,720
However, generally speaking, physiologically,

112
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:22,640
the human body does not really have a way of getting rid of iron.

113
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:28,200
And so you only want to bring in iron when it's needed by the body.

114
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:31,400
And you don't want to allow excess iron to come into the body.

115
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:37,420
And so the enterocyte plays that important role of deciding whether or not iron should go into the blood.

116
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:41,340
And it uses ferroportin to make that decision.

117
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:48,060
So ferroportin is the key transporter that takes iron from the enterocyte and places it into the blood.

118
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:51,860
Now once iron gets into the blood, it's always bound.

119
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:56,320
Remember that iron has the ability to generate free radicals, so it will never be left alone.

120
00:08:56,420 --> 00:08:57,780
It will always be bound to something.

121
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:01,160
In the blood, it's bound to a molecule called transferrin.

122
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:08,680
And transferrin transports iron and then delivers it to the liver and to the bone marrow macrophages for storage.

123
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:17,500
Once iron gets to the liver or the bone marrow macrophages, it's stored in the cell, but it's stored bound to ferritin.

124
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:23,140
So ferritin is the key molecule to which iron is bound when it is being stored.

125
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:33,500
And again, it's important to keep iron bound to ferritin because that binding prevents iron from generating free radicals via the Fenton reaction.

126
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:38,960
And we covered that in detail when we talked about free radicals in Chapter 1 of this text.

127
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:45,820
Now there are some laboratory measurements that allow you to determine the iron status of a patient.

128
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,280
And these are very high yield to be familiar with.

129
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:51,560
Both clinically they're useful and obviously on examinations.

130
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:53,000
So let's walk through them.

131
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:59,680
Now maybe it would be best to go back and just create a simple diagram so we can see what we're actually measuring.

132
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:05,560
So again, the idea here is that if you have iron in the blood, so this is the blood vessel,

133
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:09,440
iron in the blood is going to be bound to a molecule called transferrin.

134
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:15,420
Now generally speaking, for every three transferrin molecules, one, two, let's make a third one here,

135
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:21,760
for every three transferrin molecules, one of those transferrin molecules will be carrying an iron.

136
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:28,940
Eventually that iron is delivered to the macrophage, so let's call this the macrophage.

137
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:34,440
And when iron is present within the macrophage, iron is bound to ferritin.

138
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:36,920
So we'll connect it to ferritin over here.

139
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:44,200
Based on this understanding that I've sort of put here on the screen, we can measure a few pieces of information.

140
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:49,280
So for example, if we want to know how much iron is in the blood, we can measure the serum iron.

141
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:53,320
If we want to know how much iron is in the blood, we can measure the serum iron.

142
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:59,880
If we want to know how many transferrin molecules are present in the blood, whether they be bound or not doesn't matter.

143
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:03,580
If we want to know how many transferrin molecules are present in the blood,

144
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:08,320
we can measure something called the TIBC, which is the total iron binding capacity.

145
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:12,580
It tells you how many transferrin molecules are present in the blood.

146
00:11:13,080 --> 00:11:17,260
If you want to know how many of the transferrin molecules are actually bound by iron,

147
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:19,320
we can calculate a percent saturation.

148
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:27,580
Finally, if you want to know how much iron is present in the storage sites, you can measure a serum ferritin.

149
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:34,720
And so ferritin would tell us how much iron is actually present in the bone marrow macrophages and within the liver.

150
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:40,980
So these are four key measurements, and they're going to come into play as we move forward in this discussion.

151
00:11:41,660 --> 00:11:48,680
Now when we think about iron deficiency, we should understand that deficiency can be caused by a dietary lack or blood loss.

152
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:54,000
These are the two sort of common etiologies of how a patient can become iron deficient.

153
00:11:54,520 --> 00:12:01,640
And it's in the context of these two etiologies that I like to think of iron deficiency according to the individual subsets of patients.

154
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:06,440
So for example, in an infant, an important cause of iron deficiency would be breastfeeding.

155
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:10,600
And that's because breast milk contains little to no iron.

156
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:14,120
And so infants can become iron deficient when they're breastfeeding.

157
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:18,500
In a child, iron deficiency can arise if there's a poor diet.

158
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:24,260
And so children, they're growing, they need iron, and sometimes the diet is not appropriate for that growth.

159
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:29,140
And so children can become iron deficient due to poor diet.

160
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:36,360
In adults, important classic causes of iron deficiency in a male, you think about peptic ulcer disease.

161
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:39,420
That's actually the most common cause in a middle-aged male.

162
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:46,780
And the most common cause in a middle-aged female would be menorrhagia, which is heavy menstrual bleeding, in which iron would be lost because blood is lost.

163
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:47,920
Or pregnancy.

164
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:52,240
In pregnancy, a woman needs a large amount of iron to grow the fetus.

165
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:55,340
And so these are two common causes in middle-aged adults.

166
00:12:55,740 --> 00:13:01,640
In older adults, common causes in the Western world are colon polyps or carcinoma.

167
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:07,260
And in older adults, the most common cause in the developing world is actually a hookworm.

168
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:17,920
And if we go back to microbiology, recall that the two hookworms to be familiar with, one is a nicator, and the other is ancelostoma.

169
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:27,340
And so nicator and ancelostoma are two hookworms that are common causes of iron deficiency, again, because of the blood loss associated with hookworms.

170
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:31,920
These are common causes of iron deficiency in the developing world.

171
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:35,320
There are some other causes to be aware of.

172
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:44,020
Obviously, malnutrition could result in iron deficiency, but we kind of highlighted that with the notion of poor diet in a child.

173
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:47,560
Malabsorption could result in iron deficiency.

174
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:55,340
Let's say that a patient has a disease that disrupts the duodenum, like celiac disease, in which the villi of the duodenum are destroyed.

175
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:58,360
That can result in a certain degree of malabsorption.

176
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:02,480
And if there's malabsorption, the patient could obviously become iron deficient.

177
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:05,060
And then a third example would be gastrectomy.

178
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:07,240
And this actually is a kind of high-yield example.

179
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:13,940
And the reason this is high-yield is because it goes back to a little bit of biochemistry concerning iron.

180
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:17,760
And that is that iron can be present in an Fe2-plus state.

181
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:21,380
It can also be present in an Fe3-plus state.

182
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:27,820
And it's the Fe2-plus that is better, that is more easily absorbed by the body.

183
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:34,120
And the way I remember that, a little funny, but the way I remember that is Fe2 goes into the body.

184
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:36,540
Fe2 goes into the body.

185
00:14:36,540 --> 00:14:40,380
Why is that important for this discussion of gastrectomy?

186
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:44,880
Well, the idea is that acid maintains the Fe2-plus state.

187
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:50,720
And so if there is acidity, the Fe2 remains in the two, the iron remains in the two-plus state.

188
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:53,800
And by remaining in the two-plus state, it's more readily absorbed.

189
00:14:54,520 --> 00:15:00,260
Gastrectomy, where a patient would lose part of the stomach, may result in decreased acid.

190
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:05,260
And the decreased acid would result in decreased bioavailability of iron

191
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:07,880
because less iron would be in the two-plus state.

192
00:15:08,280 --> 00:15:12,280
So this becomes important, again, because it ties into a little bit of biochemistry.

193
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:17,940
When a patient becomes iron deficient, they actually go through a series of phases.

194
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:27,280
And it's nice to understand these phases or stages because they will help you to better understand what's going on in iron deficiency.

195
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:30,120
So I'm actually going to take a moment to walk you through each of these.

196
00:15:30,120 --> 00:15:36,440
Now, the very first thing, let's pretend that there's a normal patient, and they've got normal iron, and they're doing fine.

197
00:15:36,900 --> 00:15:42,760
And then let's say that the patient, I don't know, they get a hookworm infection, and so now they're starting to lose blood.

198
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:49,220
So what's going to happen is that initially, as the bone marrow is trying to make red blood cells,

199
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,900
it's going to use up the stored iron.

200
00:15:51,900 --> 00:15:57,660
That's the very first thing that it will do when there isn't sufficient iron being brought into the body.

201
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:04,440
So over time, the very early stage of iron deficiency is that the storage iron will be depleted.

202
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,440
Now recall, what is the measure of storage iron?

203
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:09,180
Ferritin.

204
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:12,140
Ferritin tells you how much iron is stored in the body.

205
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:18,960
Now, when the storage iron is depleted, what is going to happen to the ferritin, the serum ferritin?

206
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:23,380
Then the serum ferritin is going to go down, correct?

207
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:27,500
Because the storage iron is being consumed.

208
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:36,620
And as the serum ferritin goes down, one of the other things to bear in mind is that sort of always opposite of what ferritin does is TIBC.

209
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:43,400
So whenever the ferritin goes down, the total iron binding capacity is going to go up.

210
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:46,460
And what is the total iron binding capacity measuring again?

211
00:16:46,680 --> 00:16:48,520
It's measuring transferrin.

212
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,160
This sort of makes sense in my mind.

213
00:16:51,160 --> 00:16:58,360
Whenever the ferritin is down, then the macrophages and the liver will sort of recognize that the ferritin is down,

214
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:01,680
particularly because one of the storage sites of iron is the liver.

215
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:07,480
And then the liver will begin to pump out more transferrin molecules to go and try to find more iron.

216
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:14,600
And so any time the ferritin goes down, the TIBC, which is a measure of the number of transferrin molecules, will go up.

217
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:20,740
General principle, always, when the ferritin goes down, the TIBC will go up.

218
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:23,740
So that's really the first stage of iron deficiency.

219
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:31,720
Once the stored iron is used, then the next thing that's going to happen is the serum iron is going to be consumed.

220
00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:37,840
And as the serum iron is going to be consumed, a few things are going to happen according to your laboratory studies.

221
00:17:37,840 --> 00:17:45,500
Number one, the serum iron will go down because as serum iron is being used, obviously its levels are going to go down.

222
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:51,780
And number two, the percent saturation of iron will actually also go down.

223
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:54,580
Now the way to remember this is to just think about it.

224
00:17:54,660 --> 00:18:01,420
So let's remind ourselves that if you have transferrin molecules, okay, let's draw here six transferrin molecules.

225
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:05,060
So one, two, three, four, five, six.

226
00:18:05,180 --> 00:18:07,040
We've got six transferrin molecules, correct?

227
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:12,540
Now normally, when you think about transferrin, one out of every three is bound to iron.

228
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:15,560
So one out of every three is bound to iron.

229
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:16,860
So it looks like this.

230
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:21,480
Now if one out of every three is bound to iron, then what's the normal percent saturation?

231
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:26,900
Well, in this particular case, we've got two molecules of iron and six molecules of transferrin.

232
00:18:27,220 --> 00:18:32,860
So the current saturation, I'm going to draw it up here in the left-hand side of your slide, is going to be two over six,

233
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:39,940
which really equals one-third, which equals 33%, which is the normal percent saturation.

234
00:18:40,480 --> 00:18:42,980
All right, so the normal percent saturation is 33%.

235
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:48,880
Now let's assume that the patient is becoming iron deficient and the serum iron is depleted.

236
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:57,000
Well, if this iron molecule is consumed, now only one out of six transferrin molecules is bound by iron.

237
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:03,100
And so one over six equals, you know, 16%, roughly.

238
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:06,700
And so you can see that the percent saturation has gone down.

239
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:12,720
When serum iron is depleted, the serum iron will go down and the percent saturation will also go down.

240
00:19:13,060 --> 00:19:16,600
So that's the next phase of iron deficiency.

241
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:21,660
Now the third stage or phase of iron deficiency is normalcytic anemia.

242
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:22,920
So what's the idea here?

243
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:28,200
Now as iron is being depleted from the storage sites and as iron is being depleted from the serum,

244
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:31,780
the bone marrow is still going to continue to try to make red blood cells.

245
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:34,660
And in order to make red blood cells, it needs to produce heme.

246
00:19:35,020 --> 00:19:37,300
And in order to produce heme, it's going to need iron.

247
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:42,760
And so as the bone marrow begins to recognize that iron isn't available, it'll make a sacrifice.

248
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:46,280
It'll say, I prefer to make normal red blood cells.

249
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:50,540
I want nice, pretty, red, beautiful red blood cells.

250
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:54,920
However, because I can't make as many as I would like, I'll just make less.

251
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:59,180
And so initially, the patient will actually get a normalcytic anemia.

252
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:03,140
The cells will be normal in size, but there will be less of them made.

253
00:20:03,140 --> 00:20:05,480
And so the patient will actually develop a normalcytic anemia.

254
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:10,300
The very early stage of iron deficiency anemia is actually normalcytic.

255
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:14,820
And examiners like you to know that because they want you to understand the sequence of what's going on.

256
00:20:15,100 --> 00:20:17,120
And by the way, clinically, this is important as well.

257
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:22,300
Because you may find patients in your practice that are in very early stages of iron deficiency,

258
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:27,220
then you need to know that the initial stage of iron deficiency is actually a normalcytic anemia.

259
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:33,360
Now, eventually what will happen is the anemia or the iron deficiency will become so severe

260
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:40,060
that the patient will then develop the inability to produce normal red blood cells.

261
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:45,720
And so now the bone marrow will be forced to pump out cells that are smaller than normal

262
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:51,140
and that have less hemoglobin than normal and smaller than normal because they will have undergone an extra division.

263
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:56,200
And so these would be microcytic, i.e. smaller than normal, and hypochromic.

264
00:20:56,200 --> 00:21:01,040
Hypochromic means that they contain less color than they normally should have.

265
00:21:01,480 --> 00:21:04,080
And remember that when you consider the color of a red blood cell,

266
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:07,820
the normal red blood cell has a central area of pallor.

267
00:21:08,380 --> 00:21:13,860
That central area of pallor will become expanded so that more of the red blood cell

268
00:21:13,860 --> 00:21:16,980
does not have the red color of hemoglobin.

269
00:21:17,380 --> 00:21:19,740
And so you get a microcytic hypochromic anemia.

270
00:21:20,760 --> 00:21:23,200
And these are the key stages of iron deficiency.

271
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:28,320
Now, let's go through and remind ourselves of a couple clinical features of iron deficiency.

272
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:30,360
Obviously, the patients will become anemic.

273
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:34,620
Another finding, clinically, would be that the patients would have choelonychia,

274
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:40,640
which is sort of spoon-shaped nails.

275
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:42,340
And then they can have pica.

276
00:21:42,480 --> 00:21:43,160
And what's pica?

277
00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:48,100
Pica is an abnormality in which patients begin to chew on things abnormally,

278
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:50,540
like they may chew on dirt, they may chew on ice.

279
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:55,960
And the way I think about it is sort of a psychological drive to get iron from any source possible,

280
00:21:56,600 --> 00:22:00,280
chewing on dirt, perhaps seeking iron within dirt, for example.

281
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:01,640
So that's called pica.

282
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:05,460
Now, what are the key laboratory findings in iron deficiency anemia?

283
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:07,880
Well, you know that they're going to have a microcytic,

284
00:22:07,880 --> 00:22:13,800
so the MTV would be less than 80, hypochromic, the red cells will look less pink than they should,

285
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:16,120
with an increased RDW.

286
00:22:16,320 --> 00:22:18,140
Now, this requires a little bit of discussion.

287
00:22:18,500 --> 00:22:19,640
What is the RDW?

288
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:24,380
The RDW stands for the Red Blood Cell Distribution Width.

289
00:22:24,940 --> 00:22:27,020
Red Blood Cell Distribution Width.

290
00:22:27,380 --> 00:22:33,800
And basically, what the RDW measures is what is the spectrum of size of the red blood cells.

291
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:38,260
If all of the red blood cells are relatively the same size, then the RDW would be low.

292
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:43,340
And if there's a wide spectrum of red blood cell size, then the RDW would be high.

293
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:51,040
Now, in an iron deficiency anemia, recall that the initial phase of the bone marrow response to iron deficiency

294
00:22:51,040 --> 00:22:54,180
is to try to make as many normal cells as possible.

295
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:59,200
And then, once the iron deficiency progresses, it will begin to make smaller cells.

296
00:22:59,660 --> 00:23:00,980
So there's going to be a spectrum.

297
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:04,260
There's going to be some normal cells, and there's going to be some small cells.

298
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:14,440
And so that spectrum of normal cells and small cells is going to widen the distribution of the size of the red blood cells.

299
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:21,920
And so the RDW is going to increase because of this broad spectrum of varying sizes of the red blood cells.

300
00:23:22,440 --> 00:23:23,660
So that's important to know.

301
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:28,000
It's classically seen in iron deficiency anemia.

302
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:32,340
Now, patients with iron deficiency anemia, let's think about this for a minute.

303
00:23:32,340 --> 00:23:36,920
Their serum ferritin, sorry, their iron stores are going to be low, correct?

304
00:23:37,020 --> 00:23:40,180
Because they're using up the iron, so the serum ferritin will be down.

305
00:23:40,600 --> 00:23:48,160
Anytime the serum ferritin is down, the TIBC is going to be up, i.e., the liver will produce more transferrin molecules.

306
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:49,860
And so that's the measure of the TIBC.

307
00:23:49,860 --> 00:23:55,800
Again, the serum iron will go down because the serum iron will be used.

308
00:23:56,140 --> 00:23:59,640
And when the serum iron goes down, the percent saturation will also go down.

309
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:04,020
This I've kind of explained previously, so we don't need much detail here.

310
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:10,240
One aspect of lab findings that I haven't talked about is the free erythrocyte protoporphyrin.

311
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:13,620
And this actually is very high yield to be familiar with.

312
00:24:13,740 --> 00:24:17,060
And it's actually a decent test for iron deficiency anemia.

313
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:23,680
And it's also a very good examiner's question because it takes you back to a little bit of biochemistry.

314
00:24:24,200 --> 00:24:28,240
Now, what do I mean by the free erythrocyte protoporphyrin?

315
00:24:28,820 --> 00:24:35,420
Recall that basically heme, correct, this is heme, heme consists of two components.

316
00:24:35,420 --> 00:24:39,720
It consists of iron plus protoporphyrin.

317
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:44,980
In an iron deficiency anemia, the iron is down.

318
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:50,700
But is there any problem in iron deficiency anemia with the production of protoporphyrin?

319
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:51,920
The answer is no.

320
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:53,540
The protoporphyrin is normal.

321
00:24:54,040 --> 00:24:58,180
And so if iron is down and the protoporphyrin is normal,

322
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:02,340
some of the protoporphyrin in the red blood cell will not be bound to iron.

323
00:25:02,500 --> 00:25:06,520
It will be floating around free because there isn't iron to bind it to actually produce heme.

324
00:25:06,520 --> 00:25:11,920
And so in the red blood cell, there will be free erythrocyte protoporphyrin.

325
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:13,840
And so that, again, is very high yield.

326
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:15,760
One, clinically it's useful.

327
00:25:16,140 --> 00:25:22,240
And two, it allows examiners to test you on some integrated biochemistry

328
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:25,780
in the context of clinical findings and pathology.

329
00:25:26,280 --> 00:25:27,820
So this is something to be well aware of.

330
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:31,580
Here's a classic blood smear from a patient with iron deficiency anemia.

331
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,200
And there's a few things to remind ourselves here.

332
00:25:34,200 --> 00:25:35,800
Number one, this is a lymphocyte.

333
00:25:36,420 --> 00:25:41,460
And the nucleus of the lymphocyte should represent the normal size of the red blood cell.

334
00:25:41,580 --> 00:25:46,640
So red blood cells should normally be about the size of the nucleus of the lymphocyte.

335
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:50,780
And in this particular case, we can see that the red blood cells are small.

336
00:25:51,260 --> 00:25:53,300
And some of them may be close to normal.

337
00:25:53,620 --> 00:25:54,560
But there is a spectrum.

338
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:57,620
But there certainly are an increased number of small red blood cells.

339
00:25:58,660 --> 00:26:03,720
And so this picture is highlighting for you a microcytic hypochromic anemia,

340
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:08,020
which would classically be the description of an iron deficiency anemia.

341
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:12,320
Treatment of iron deficiency anemia is very straightforward.

342
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:13,860
We give ferrous sulfate.

343
00:26:13,980 --> 00:26:17,000
And the idea here is that we want to replace the iron.

344
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:24,000
However, remember that if there's some underlying cause that would explain the patient's iron deficiency anemia,

345
00:26:24,220 --> 00:26:25,440
you need to treat that as well.

346
00:26:26,080 --> 00:26:30,800
So, for example, if there's an older male and he has iron deficiency anemia,

347
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:35,880
it's very important to rule out colonic carcinoma in that patient.

348
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:40,540
So we don't simply just sort of figure out that there's an iron deficiency anemia and then give iron.

349
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:42,560
We need to ask the question, why?

350
00:26:42,560 --> 00:26:44,140
Why is the patient iron deficient?

351
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:48,720
And that's important because if there's a cancer, we certainly want to detect it and treat that as well.

352
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:53,080
One associated syndrome to be familiar with, it's relatively high yield,

353
00:26:53,460 --> 00:26:56,060
and so we're going to throw it in the context of this discussion.

354
00:26:56,300 --> 00:26:58,440
It's called the Plummer-Vincent syndrome.

355
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:07,860
This is iron deficiency anemia that has an additional association of esophageal web and atrophic glossitis,

356
00:27:07,860 --> 00:27:12,680
such that the patients present with anemia, dysphagia, and a beefy red tongue.

357
00:27:13,060 --> 00:27:17,640
Let me just highlight what an esophageal web is to make this discussion a little more clear.

358
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:19,180
This is the esophagus.

359
00:27:19,300 --> 00:27:21,500
You know that the esophagus is lined by a mucosa,

360
00:27:21,960 --> 00:27:25,980
and the mucosa would be relatively tight up against the lumen of the esophagus.

361
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:31,440
However, there can be redundancy in the mucosa so that some of the mucosa outfolds in this way,

362
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:37,500
and that could potentially create an obstruction or a partial obstruction within the esophagus.

363
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:44,340
And so, for example, if a patient were to try to consume foods, in particular poorly chewed foods,

364
00:27:44,760 --> 00:27:51,480
then these foods could actually get stuck as it's trying to move down the esophagus, resulting in dysphagia.

365
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:55,700
So this mucosal protrusion is called an esophageal web,

366
00:27:55,700 --> 00:27:58,500
and esophageal webs can be seen in many circumstances,

367
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:04,080
but they're highly associated with Plummer-Vincent syndrome, especially on examinations.

